  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tonight into tomorrow morning, calm winds will \nbring a fog threat away from the coast, which could be locally \ndense just before/around sunrise. Next appreciable chance of \nrain arrives Mon and Tue as yet another frontal boundary pushes \nsouthward. For Mon, the rain chances are confined to mainly NW \nzones as the front has trended slower and later in the day. Tue \nappears the best chance for widespread showers and embedded \nthunderstorms as PW's surge to aoa 2\" and frontal boundary is \ndraped acrs ENC, and thus pops raised to 60-70%.\n\nKey Message 2...By mid week, strong high pressure system \nbecomes anchored acrs New England and ern Canada, while at the \nsame time broad low pres develops off the Mid Atlantic coast, \ntightening the pressure gradient along the mid- atlantic and \nnortheast. This setup will usher in stronger NE winds into\ncoastal ENC. Exact timing, placement, and strength of the low\nwill dictate wind speeds and impacts to coastal ENC. Ensemble\nprobs still show potential for 20-30mph sustained winds, gusting\nto 40-50mph along OBX. This however is highly variable as it\ndepends on the low. What is certain is that a long fetch of \ntightened nerly gradient winds offshore will bring high wave \nenergy towards vulnerable portions of the Outer Banks. It is too\nsoon to determine specifics at this point and trends will have \nto be monitored in the coming days. There is high confidence in\nover 9 foot waves at over 9 second periods impacting the Outer\nBanks late this week.",
